The test passes but I have seen from the coverage perspective, arrList.Contains is not covered (it's used in the Assumption). So, my question is why is it that the Contains method is not covered in my test? Is there a way to incorporate that method in the test and cover it?
UPDATE:
I just noticed PexObserve.ValueForViewing doesn't actually do anything. Perhaps this could be a reason why Contains is not covered.

A: An exploratory testing session shows that Pex does not support coverage for private fields and methods. It only covers public fields and methods. If the Contains method is private, Pex wouldn't be able to cover it.
From the PexPoint team on UserVoice,

In a nutshell, all public, instance methods, constructors, properties, fields on value types and reference types are covered by default. However, if you have methods that are hidden (private, internal) or sealed, your tests will not cover them. I have to say this is a feature not a bug: not all code ends up in tests.

Additionally, Pex assumes its subjects are thread-safe. If your ArrayList code is not thread-safe, Pex tests may not fully exercise your code.

Pex isn't designed to fully understand all behaviors of a system. If a system is thread-unsafe, or behaves differently than anticipated in your tests, that's a sign that you should write more tests around the system's edge-cases. It's not about covering all possibilities; it's about being wary of common bugs, and being comfortable with the system's behavior.

So it's reasonable that Contains method wouldn't be covered if it's private. A possible workaround is to make sure to use public methods in your tests.
Also, adding your assertion inline with the test might help improve its coverage. Let me know if that helps too.

A: Covering private methods/fields in Pex is not currently supported and this seems to be the issue you are encountering. You might want to send feedback to Pex team about this and they would certainly investigate it.
As for including Contains method, you could make it public. Another way is to introduce a public wrapper method around it which you test with Pex. This way you will cover both private and public methods. Here's a simple example:

public class MyArrayList
{
    private ArrayList realList = new ArrayList();

    public bool Contains(object value)
    {
        return realList.Contains(value);
    }

    public void Add(object value)
    {
        realList.Add(value);
    }

    // other public methods...
}

Now you have your real list in a wrapped class which you may test with Pex, and it will cover both private and public (actually, wrapping) methods.

Regarding PexObserve.ValueForViewing, it seems they are intended to be for logging/debugging but Pex appears to have issues with them in certain scenarios. This might be the reason why Contains is not covered, or it could not appear in the code coverage report because it doesn't really contribute to the execution of the code (it just reads the state, not modifying it). I would advise avoiding such methods as much as possible for Pex testing, as their effectiveness can vary for different scenarios.

For testing, I would suggest considering a different systematic testing method as Pex tends to be exploratory, which could impact your coverage: use something more structured, like NUnit or xUnit, and use asserts to validate your expectations. 

Remember, Pex is to be used responsibly. It should not be taken as the canonical way of testing your code, but rather as a way to help ensure that your code behaves as expected in an edge scenario or when an unexpected pathway is taken in your code.

Hope that helps,

I hope this would help guide you to an effective approach when working with Pex.


A: The answer is that Pex generates a randomized test which is more likely to find buggy paths. It does not guarantee to find all the bugs in the actual deployment system. It is primarily designed as a "quick win" automated test method, not as a substitute for a thorough manual or automated regression suite. 
It will not and cannot fully and reliably cover all code paths not only because of the restrictions on Pex, but also, because--------------------
