Here, you can see some assumptions that might apply to all the test cases; and then an Assert that verifies a certain condition is true about the final state of the list right after the operation.

In this case, we are looking at the IndexOf method, we've also observed some properties of the original and new list before and after performing the operation. This will allow you to quickly check if your assumptions and conditions hold true. 

Adding the assertion helps by directly giving us information about the method's results. We can also use other methods like `PexObserve` and `Assume` as needed, as they become more and more useful as your tests get more complex. 

For example, PexObserve allows you to view values that might aid in verifying your tests, and Assume allows you to include preconditions, which might be necessary in your data.  

Finally, as long as these concepts and techniques are applicable to Microsoft's testing tools, they will be applicable to tools like Pex.

Remember, the purpose of these exercises is to learn, not to be afraid of testing. If a test comes out red, it means you are doing something wrong. Take it as a chance to learn about your code more thoroughly. The point is, it will make you a better software engineer. 

[Further reading:](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/automated-testing-of-software/)
- M. Wagner, D. Lill et al., "Automated Testing of Software: A Comprehensive Review.” Information and Software Technology 55 (2013)
- N. Broschart, B. Hermanns, M. Wagner, and D. Lill, "Automated Testing of Software Widgets." Journal of Systems and Software 86 (2010)

These articles and other resources can provide a deeper understanding of automated testing and how it fits into the larger picture of software quality and assurance.
  
It's great to be learning from these kinds of exercises, so don’t hesitate to take them with a grain of salt. And when the time comes, take them all in- stricto sensus. 
- Robbie

