In the above Pex code, I would like to make an observation for the elements of arrayList using PexObserve.ValueForViewing method. This observation code is supposed to show the count values of arrayList before and after the operation - which I have done. However, I would like to also show "LastIndexOf(x)" and "Contains(x)" values too, before and after insert operation. For that purpose, I have used PexObserve.ValueForViewing. And here is my question:

How can I also observe other properties of ArrayList using PexObserve.ValueForViewing,

For example, how can I get the Capacity property?

To know the count of elements x that I inserted at the beginning of the test?

To see the maximum capacity of arrayList object

Any help will be greatly appreciated.


A: It is not possible to get or observe properties other than public properties of objects in the array list directly using PexObserve.ValueForViewing. The Pex Observer currently supports viewing of public and non-public fields and properties, but it does not expose all properties and fields of a class including system generated fields of a class. 
To track other properties, you have to manually log them before and after the operation.
For example
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Capacity", Old_arrList_Capacity);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Capacity", New_arrList_Capacity);

where you define Old_arrList_Capacity and New_arrList_Capacity yourself before and after the call to 'capacity' property of ArrayList object.
ArrayList arrList;
....
int Old_arrList_Capacity = arrList.Capacity;
....
arrList.Add(some_value);
....
int New_arrList_Capacity = arrList.Capacity;
....

Similarly for Count of x and maximum capacity.
I would suggest to use the PexSettings.EnableStatePropagation = false; for your arrayList in the test method and enable it back for your remaining code. This will help in observing changes in your internal data members of the object.
Hope i have answered your question.
Regards,
Chaitu

A: You can see the capacity when you add items to the list by using arrList.Capacity.
This is how you can get the count of the items x:


*

*If x is a fixed value, you could count occurrences using LINQ - arrList.Count(i => i == x); 

*If x varies randomly, you could use a different data member to count occurrences in the same test.


For the maximum capacity of arrayList object, ArrayList.MaxCapacity property will tell you the maximum number that the ArrayList can accommodate without resizing, though it's not recommended to change this property as it's not meant to be used by the developers.
For these and other properties/features of ArrayList, Pex currently does not support checking with PexObserve or PexAssume as it is not a standard part of the public API. If you know the logic to obtain these properties, you can write your own pre-condition and post-condition checks using Debug.Assert.
Hope this help.
Regards,
Hitesh

A: Here is an observation to the capacity using PexObserve.
 [PexMethod]
    [PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
    public void TestAddingOverCapacity(ArrayList list, int val)
    {
        //Arrange
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$BeforeCount", list.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$BeforeCapacity", list.Capacity);

        //Act
        list.Add(val);

        //Assert
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$AfterCount", list.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$AfterCapacity", list.Capacity);
    }

This is observing the count and capacity before and after the addition of a new item to the list. The Add method is expected to throw an exception if the capacity is exceeded. In theory, it should double the current capacity if no space is available and still throw an exception.
This way you can observe how a list capacity grows during the tests. But--------------------            AssertThat.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

            if (Old_arrListContainsX)
            {
                // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ New index of x is not greater than old one
                AssertThat.IsTrue(New_Ret <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

                // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ New size of list is same as the old one
                AssertThat.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

            }
            else
            {
                // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ New size of list is one more than old one
                AssertThat.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);

            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this test, a number of assertions are used to confirm that the outcome of the `IndexOf` method (when it successfully returns an index, or -1 when the number is not in the array) behaves as expected.

This contract tests the `IndexOf` property of the class under test—ArrayList. Necessary information, such as the initial state of the ArrayList before an invocation, the return value of the method under test, and the new state of the ArrayList after the invocation, are stored for viewing. 

If the precondition holds (the original list contained at least one occurrence of the item being searched for), then the postcondition should hold (the returned index should be equal to or greater than the last occurrence before any operation). The size of the ArrayList remains the same.

If the precondition does not hold (the original list does not contain the item), then the postcondition will also not hold (even though a 'successful' (i.e., no exception is thrown) return value does not guarantee that item will be in the collection), and the size of the list might change. This test case also confirms behavior in the event that the item is added to the ArrayList again.

Other possible conditions aren't explicitly covered, but the test case does demonstrate the fundamental principle of Pex-driven testing—ensuring each contract about your method holds true for any invocation. These contract tests ensure the correctness of these contracts. 

#### Partial Property Test Cases

You specify a test method as a partial test by annotating it with the `[PexMethod]` attribute. Test method names should be written in PascalCase.

``` csharp
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest 
    { 
        [PexMethod]
        public void MyArrayListTest(ArrayList arrList) 
        { 
            //test code here
        }
    }
```

Here is a typical contract for this test.

``` csharp

using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void MyArrayListTest([PexAssumeValid] ArrayList arrList) 
        {
            bool Old_arrListIsEmpty = arrList.Count == 0;

            arrList.Add(1);

            bool New_arrListIsEmpty = arrList.Count == 0;
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            Assume.That<bool>(!New_arrListIsEmpty);

            arrList.RemoveAt(0);
            arrList.TrimToSize();

            int New_arrListCountAfterRemove = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_isEmpty", Old_arrListIsEmpty);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_isEmpty", New_arrListIsEmpty);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count_After_Remove", New_arrListCountAfterRemove);

            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListIsEmpty);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCountAfterRemove);
        }
    }
}
```

This test is checking that an item has been added to the ArrayList, that ArrayList is trimmed to have its capacity match its size, and that the count of the ArrayList is reduced by one post-rem--------------------            if (New_Ret < 0) 
            {
            Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX);
            }

            // assertion that if the old list contains x, then the returned index is less than or equal to LastIndexOf x
            if (Old_arrListContainsX)
            {
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	     }

	    // assertion that Count hasn't increased after the operation 
	    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
	}
    }
}

For these two methods, we would test that the count remains the same and that the methods return the correct value. We also test that certain state values of the array list remain consistent. 

You might notice that the conditions for the assertions are specified using Debug.Assert statements which cause the debugger to break when an Assert condition is met. This is a way of catching unexpected failures in the program under test.

We are also using the PexObserve utility to record values for viewing when PEX is run. This can be useful when generating coverage data to see what tests are being reached.

The Assert class from the NUnit framework ensures that certain conditions will always be true if they are not, the test will stop and report an error.

Lastly, PexAction attribute is used to tell PEX what action(s) to perform. For the IndexOf method, the PexAction attribute is required because the method does not have a return value that we can check. Therefore, we need to tell PEX to perform the IndexOf method.
 
Also, please note that the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is very important because it instructs Pex to focus on testing the code under this attribute. The purpose is to ensure that every operation in the code under test can be tested by Pex.

So, the conclusion for these contracts is that they ensure that the method IndexOf and LastIndexOf behave correctly and maintain the properties of the array list when used with the ArrayList object. The contract ensures that the list does not contain the element if the return value is negative and that the count value stays the same.

I hope that gives a clear understanding of what the contracts are all about.

